# hui ES CIRCUIT SCRAPPER on a 2P dummy (14z-131). The 63214 command
# grab with TWO PUNCHES (buttons 1+2) instead of one — the ES version the
# maintainer names as historically problematic alongside the MP/HP one
# (2026-09-04: "these have all had their share of corrections, especially
# circuit scrapper and ES circuit scrapper, and even now I am not 100% sure
# they are identical both mechanically and visually to their VS2 versions").
#
# AUTHORED AS REPLAY 80 WITH ONE TOKEN CHANGED: `L2` -> `L12` at both grab
# attempts. Everything else — the walk-in, the early-window pokes, the 2P
# dummy — is byte-identical to 80, so a difference between the two replays
# is the ES button and nothing else.
#
# *** THIS REPLAY REQUIRES METER OR IT IS NOT AN ES RIG AT ALL. ***
# With an empty stock the ES input DEGRADES SILENTLY to the ordinary MP
# command grab and the run looks perfectly healthy — measured 14z-131: without
# a stock poke, replay 97 returns byte-identical numbers to replay 80 (same 16
# offsets, same pose sequence, same 19 damage, stock 0 throughout). That is
# the [VSP-131] trap in its purest form, and the [VSP-123] rule for MODES
# applies: poke the resource and ASSERT it was spent.
# So every user of this replay must poke P1's stock (`2900:ff8509:09;
# 3000:ff8509:09;3100:ff8509:09`) and assert the DISCRIMINATOR: the stock
# DROPS 9 -> 8 at f3152, the grab frame. Replay 80 under the identical poke
# never drops it. Do not read a green run that did not show the drop.
#
# Used by tests/audit_tenant_throw_geometry.sh, which compares the VICTIM's
# hold positions and post-release arc against native vsav2. Pokes are
# EARLY-WINDOW ONLY (1400-1500) for the reason replay 80's header gives:
# late pokes leak into the 2P commit/load and turn P2 into a variant id.
